1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,689
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,148 --> 00:00:06,270
Today we will talk about one of the earthlings' favourite things: Food.

3
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:44,510
Like many organisms, earthlings need various chemicals to sustain their metabolism.

4
00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:49,250
These chemicals are taken in three ways: Breathing, drinking and eating.

5
00:00:50,070 --> 00:00:55,670
Breathing means aspirating oxygen and exhaling carbon dioxide, which is heavier than oxygen.

6
00:00:55,670 --> 00:01:04,210
So, earthlings constantly lose weight through breathing, approximately half a kilogram per day, which is of course replaced by eating and drinking.

7
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:08,030
Eating means taking aliments through the mouth.

8
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:12,310
The mouth has two purposes: Shredding the food, and analyzing it.

9
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:16,944
Drinking is basically the same, but with liquids - mainly hydric acid.

10
00:01:17,706 --> 00:01:24,126
Besides hydric acid, there are three main components of food: Carbohydrates, fat and proteins.

11
00:01:26,126 --> 00:01:28,442
Carbohydrates are the main energy source of the body.

12
00:01:28,883 --> 00:01:34,183
Remember sugar, the energy-rich molecules produced by plants out of sunlight and thin air?

13
00:01:34,463 --> 00:01:37,923
The simplest sugars are glucose, fructose and sucrose.

14
00:01:38,603 --> 00:01:46,783
Glucose looks a bit like a Centauri donut with a little flag, fructose like a smaller donut with two little flags, and sucrose like the two of them glued together.

15
00:01:46,783 --> 00:01:50,303
There are also non-circular variants of those molecules.

16
00:01:51,323 --> 00:01:56,223
Now, carbohydrates are basically sugar, and everything you can assemble from sugar.

17
00:01:56,463 --> 00:02:06,063
For example, when you create a glucose chain and put all the little flags in the same direction, you get starch, a good energy source - you find it, for example, in bread.

18
00:02:06,063 --> 00:02:13,123
But when you alternate the orientation of the flags, you get cellulose, which is indigestible for earth creatures.

19
00:02:13,843 --> 00:02:15,743
Except for the microbes.

20
00:02:18,583 --> 00:02:25,743
Even cellulose-eating animals like cows can't digest it by themselves; they have it digested by microbes in their bowels.

21
00:02:26,483 --> 00:02:31,478
A special kind of cellulose structure is wood - the stuff the stems of trees are made of.

22
00:02:31,819 --> 00:02:35,136
It's basically cellulose fibers held together by a matrix of lignin.

23
00:02:35,804 --> 00:02:40,909
Wood is not edible either, but it plays an important part in the development of earthling alimentation.

24
00:02:41,171 --> 00:02:42,871
We will come back to this later.

25
00:02:43,371 --> 00:02:46,538
The second component of earthling food are fats.

26
00:02:46,901 --> 00:02:47,887
What is fat?

27
00:02:48,249 --> 00:02:55,109
Well, fat molecules look a bit like fat little baby squids, with a body of glycerol and three tentacles of fatty acids.

28
00:02:55,109 --> 00:03:01,929
Missing hydrogen atoms in the tentacles create kinks, in this case we speak of unsaturated fatty acids.

29
00:03:02,529 --> 00:03:08,710
Anyway, creatures use fat principally to store energy, for example to survive the cold season when food is rare.

30
00:03:09,191 --> 00:03:13,327
Predators, and earthlings in particular, love eating fat of other creatures.

31
00:03:13,854 --> 00:03:19,269
In rich countries, people have nowadays so much to eat that they often accumulate too much fat.

32
00:03:19,269 --> 00:03:27,649
They try to burn fat with physical exercises - when the body burns fat, it produces more carbon dioxide, thus loses more weight.

33
00:03:28,009 --> 00:03:36,489
A less exhausting way of burning fat would probably be to simply turn the heating down - when it's cold, the earthling body consumes energy to maintain its temperature.

34
00:03:37,229 --> 00:03:39,609
Strangely, this method is not very popular.

35
00:03:40,849 --> 00:03:43,874
Now to the third component of food: proteins.

36
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:51,684
Those are complex molecular nanomachines which basically run the organism on the molecular level, from muscle power to transmitting messages.

37
00:03:52,207 --> 00:04:01,747
An important kind of proteins are those little nanobots called enzymes which catalyse all kinds of chemical reactions, including complex tasks like copying the genetic code.

38
00:04:02,607 --> 00:04:06,642
The blueprints for these protein-nanobots are all encoded in the DNA.

39
00:04:07,287 --> 00:04:12,232
But how can a one-dimensional genetic code contain blueprints of three-dimensional nanomachines?

40
00:04:12,614 --> 00:04:17,757
Well, all proteins are made of only twenty basic building blocks called amino acids.

41
00:04:18,197 --> 00:04:21,357
Those building blocks can be plugged together to build long chains.

42
00:04:21,917 --> 00:04:31,117
That's exactly how proteins are built: The DNA sequences are translated into amino acid chains, with three base pairs corresponding to one type of amino acid.

43
00:04:31,117 --> 00:04:38,077
Once the chain is built, it naturally curls together to some wibbly wobbly curly whirly thing, the actual protein.

44
00:04:39,057 --> 00:04:52,677
Now, some of these basic building blocks can't be synthesized by the earthling body, they need to be taken from plants and animals - either by eating protein packages they produce for their offspring, or by eating their body, especially muscle tissue.

45
00:04:52,997 --> 00:04:55,977
Those building blocks are called essential amino acids.

46
00:04:58,337 --> 00:04:59,577
Strategic advice.

47
00:05:00,817 --> 00:05:05,310
Earthlings eat a lot of meat, and most of the meat comes from tamed animals like cows.

48
00:05:05,814 --> 00:05:09,714
So you might have the idea of weakening earthlings with a massive abduction of cows.

49
00:05:11,674 --> 00:05:21,214
However, this tactic might backfire on the long run: Many earthlings nutritionists and ecologists think that modern earthlings eat far too much meat, especially in rich countries.

50
00:05:24,114 --> 00:05:28,814
So, when you abduct most of the cows, you might actually do mankind a favour.

51
00:05:30,154 --> 00:05:42,594
Besides hydric acid, carbohydrates, fat and proteins, earthlings need other things in smaller quantities, for example iron, calcium, vitamins and sodium chloride, aka salt.

52
00:05:43,534 --> 00:05:49,734
Salt is an ionic compound, held together not by an electron sharing deal, but an electron lending deal.

53
00:05:50,094 --> 00:05:55,117
A sodium atom lends an electron to a chlorine atom, so they have both a complete outer shell.

54
00:05:55,117 --> 00:06:00,617
This makes both atoms electrically charged, in other words ions, so they stick together.

55
00:06:00,917 --> 00:06:02,599
This is called an ionic bond.

56
00:06:02,999 --> 00:06:05,539
Salt is very important for the earthling metabolism.

57
00:06:06,319 --> 00:06:10,159
Some food, on the other side, is dangerous for the body.

58
00:06:10,439 --> 00:06:17,899
This holds particularly for food infested or altered by tiny food rivals or parasites like microbes, insects or fungi.

59
00:06:18,199 --> 00:06:20,239
This kind of food is called rotten.

60
00:06:20,979 --> 00:06:27,179
To distinguish good food from bad or rotten food, earthlings need a way to analyze food before eating it.

61
00:06:27,419 --> 00:06:29,507
That's the purpose of smell and taste.

62
00:06:29,507 --> 00:06:36,547
The nose and the mouth contain arrays of chemical sensors called chemo-receptors which are linked to the brain's reward system.

63
00:06:37,087 --> 00:06:43,647
Basically, "good" food causes pleasure and the urge to swallow, "bad" food creates displeasure and the urge to spit it out.

64
00:06:44,507 --> 00:06:52,927
The pleasure is also modified depending on the current needs of the body - after eating six animal bodies, the seventh one just doesn't taste so good anymore.

65
00:06:53,927 --> 00:07:00,607
Also, finding food pleasurable can be learned - many earthlings have learned to appreciate rotten food like cheese or beer.

66
00:07:01,087 --> 00:07:02,667
We will come back to this in a moment.

67
00:07:03,187 --> 00:07:06,447
Food appreciation is also transmitted from generation to generation.

68
00:07:06,967 --> 00:07:08,577
This is called food culture.

69
00:07:09,086 --> 00:07:14,966
There are basically five kinds of taste: sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami.

70
00:07:15,626 --> 00:07:21,846
Sweet is the simplest one, a pleasing sensation which indicates the earthlings preferred energy source: sugar.

71
00:07:22,566 --> 00:07:27,679
Sour indicates acids like citric acid or Vitamin C, which you find in many fruits.

72
00:07:28,545 --> 00:07:33,985
Salty indicates primarily the presence of sodium ions, in particular from sodium chloride.

73
00:07:35,045 --> 00:07:40,146
Bitter is a generally displeasing, alarming taste which may hint at rotten or poisonous food.

74
00:07:40,789 --> 00:07:45,069
The last one, umami, indicates a compound called glutamate.

75
00:07:45,069 --> 00:07:48,489
It can be found in many types of food and is generally perceived as savoury.

76
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:54,509
There might be a sixth taste, which indicates fat, but for now that's just a hypothesis.

77
00:07:55,229 --> 00:08:08,189
Interestingly, some food acts not on the taste receptors but on the temperature receptors in the mouth: Mint oil, for example, is perceived as cool, whereas so-called "spicy" food triggers the heat receptors and may even be perceived as painful.

78
00:08:08,189 --> 00:08:16,169
There is a common misconception about taste: Many earthlings believe that receptors for different tastes are located in different zones of the tongue.

79
00:08:16,589 --> 00:08:23,909
This is easy to disprove, especially for earthlings - just put something sweet onto the back of your tongue, or something bitter onto the tip.

80
00:08:24,669 --> 00:08:29,789
Nevertheless, many earthlings keep on believing this nonsense rather than trying it out for themselves.

81
00:08:30,429 --> 00:08:33,530
Now, where do the earthlings get all the edible stuff?

82
00:08:33,854 --> 00:08:42,674
At dawn of mankind, they got it essentially the same way as animals: By hunting animals and gathering fruits and seeds, and devouring the edible parts.

83
00:08:43,114 --> 00:08:47,736
But at some point, they made an important discovery: Artificial external digestion.

84
00:08:48,258 --> 00:08:51,443
This is similar to methods used by other races in the galaxy.

85
00:08:51,443 --> 00:08:58,763
The Snuglings of Karaxi-2, for example, live under a thick cloud cover protecting them from the deadly radiation of their sun.

86
00:08:59,143 --> 00:09:04,623
They feed on creatures called frooks, but some kinds of Frooks are hard to kill and difficult to digest.

87
00:09:04,943 --> 00:09:10,599
So the Snuglings catapult the frooks above the cloud cover where they are roasted by the gamma rays of the sun.

88
00:09:10,860 --> 00:09:14,800
When they fall down, they are easier to digest, and also, dead.

89
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:23,220
Some 400000 solar cycles ago, Earthlings discovered a similar method to make food digestible: Fire!

90
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:28,203
Remember wood, this hardened cellulose structure?

91
00:09:28,565 --> 00:09:33,045
Dry wood combusts easily, and it's not hard to build a fire out of wood pieces.

92
00:09:33,805 --> 00:09:47,765
Originally, earthlings used fire to keep warm and drive away predators, but some 250000 solar cycles ago they discovered that some food, in particular meat, changes its taste and consistency when exposed to fire for a while.

93
00:09:48,085 --> 00:09:49,485
This is called roasting.

94
00:09:50,365 --> 00:09:52,465
But why do earthlings do that?

95
00:09:52,825 --> 00:09:55,125
Well, there are several reasons for roasting.

96
00:09:55,745 --> 00:09:58,965
First, the heat kills most of the microbes in the food.

97
00:09:59,405 --> 00:10:07,140
Secondly, the temperature breaks down molecules like starch and unfolds some proteins, which makes the food easier to chew and easier to digest.

98
00:10:07,661 --> 00:10:13,061
Basically, roasting food is some kind of external pre-digestion, using the chemical energy in wood.

99
00:10:14,221 --> 00:10:18,183
Now, when roasting food, it often gets a brown crispy crust.

100
00:10:18,605 --> 00:10:21,045
What happens here is called "Maillard reaction".

101
00:10:21,505 --> 00:10:24,860
Basically, it's a reaction between sugar and amino acids.

102
00:10:25,422 --> 00:10:27,261
Earthlings love the taste of this crust.

103
00:10:27,772 --> 00:10:33,052
By the way, many earthlings think that roasting meat "seals in the juices" - but that is nonsense.

104
00:10:33,832 --> 00:10:39,090
Now, another way of heating food is boiling it - exposing it to boiling hydric acid.

105
00:10:39,090 --> 00:10:46,110
Boiling differs from roasting in that the temperature doesn't exceed the boiling point of hydric acid, and there is no Maillard reaction.

106
00:10:46,570 --> 00:10:51,130
Basically, it's just another variant of the same external pre-digestion process.

107
00:10:52,110 --> 00:10:57,055
Boiling and roasting are part of what earthlings do with their food before they eat it: Cooking.

108
00:10:57,437 --> 00:11:02,407
And earthlings wouldn't be earthlings if they hadn't developed cooking into an elaborate part of their culture.

109
00:11:02,834 --> 00:11:09,753
Cooking also includes cutting and slicing food, mixing and combining different ingredients, and spicing the food.

110
00:11:09,753 --> 00:11:21,314
Spicing means adding ingredients that alter the taste, mostly to increase the pleasure of eating: Yet another example of earthlings tricking out their brains' reward system to get biologically pointless pleasure.

111
00:11:22,173 --> 00:11:23,626
Tips for tourists.

112
00:11:23,626 --> 00:11:28,037
If you have a hydrocarbon-based metabolism, you might visit a restaurant.

113
00:11:28,538 --> 00:11:33,218
Here you can observe earthlings' eating habits, and also taste some food for yourself.

114
00:11:33,738 --> 00:11:38,358
However, beware of earthling drinks, they have often surprising effects.

115
00:11:39,298 --> 00:11:47,927
Some 10000 solar cycles ago, a second discovery was made: Earthlings all over the planet started cultivating food and herding animals.

116
00:11:47,927 --> 00:11:55,987
Basically, they started acting like small-scale bio administrators, rearing new species of plants and animals according to their needs.

117
00:11:56,547 --> 00:11:57,782
This is called agriculture.

118
00:11:58,410 --> 00:12:03,048
Nowadays it has grown to an industrial scale food production able to feed seven billion earthlings.

119
00:12:03,409 --> 00:12:04,789
Well, almost.

120
00:12:05,609 --> 00:12:11,334
One of the strangest aspects of food culture is the earthling habit of letting food rot in a controlled manner.

121
00:12:11,756 --> 00:12:14,877
One example is alcoholic beverages like wine or beer.

122
00:12:14,877 --> 00:12:19,317
Wine, for example, is rotten juice from fruits called grapes.

123
00:12:20,057 --> 00:12:24,837
Alcoholic beverages have a particular effect on the brain - we will talk about this in another episode.

124
00:12:25,797 --> 00:12:33,997
Another example for deliberately rotten food are products like yoghurt or cheese: Milk, altered by microbes, a process that can take months.

125
00:12:34,517 --> 00:12:38,312
Cheese is sometimes even exposed to fungi which make it rot even more.

126
00:12:38,312 --> 00:12:45,112
This process transforms the simple taste of milk into a more complex taste, sometimes even with hints of bitterness.

127
00:12:46,312 --> 00:12:57,132
Generally, eating is a highly ritualized, social activity: Social groups eat together, friends bond by sharing a meal, and mating rituals often involve eating together in a public place.

128
00:12:57,992 --> 00:13:03,932
But despite the sophisticated rituals, earthlings still keep some habits from their cave-dwelling ancestors.

129
00:13:04,832 --> 00:13:13,612
I have observed several earthling families where the mother cooks, but calls the father to cut the roast - in prehistoric times, the job of the tribe chieftain.

130
00:13:14,072 --> 00:13:19,492
Also, many men like to roast meat over open fire, just like mammoth hunters in the olden days.

131
00:13:21,212 --> 00:13:27,806
Strangely, earthlings often separate "sweet" sugar snacks from larger "salty" meals which contain all kinds of nutrients.

132
00:13:28,290 --> 00:13:37,430
The reason might be that both meals have different functions: The sugar snacks give a quick energy boost, which is often enhanced by stimulating drugs like coffee or cigarettes.

133
00:13:38,810 --> 00:13:44,670
The salty meals, on the other hand, provide long-term supply with all kinds of nutrients.

134
00:13:45,430 --> 00:13:52,772
Sometimes, the "salty" meal is followed by a sweet snack, possibly in order to provide the energy boost needed to digest said meal.

135
00:13:53,152 --> 00:13:54,372
This is called a dessert.

136
00:13:56,632 --> 00:13:57,610
Scientific advice.

137
00:13:58,129 --> 00:14:03,175
A classic alien textbook explains that you can use food to condition earthling cubs.

138
00:14:03,397 --> 00:14:11,975
Show them a series of images: When they hit a "like" button, they get some chocolate, when they hit a dislike button they get spinach, a vegetable many cubs hate.

139
00:14:11,975 --> 00:14:15,535
As a result, they will start liking every single image.

140
00:14:17,815 --> 00:14:26,795
However, this experiment has been done so many times that nowadays, countless former abductees feel the compulsive desire to hit "like" buttons all day long.

141
00:14:27,195 --> 00:14:36,538
Moreover, many victims feel the compulsive urge to use the word, "like", as often as possible - like, in every sentence, like, all day long.

142
00:14:36,538 --> 00:14:45,502
If I were like an earthling who could have like feelings, I would be like sad for these poor abductees, as they are like linguistically handicapped for like the rest of their life.

143
00:14:45,822 --> 00:14:53,686
A word to my earthling viewers: Rest assured, if you feel the urge to click on "like" right now, it does not necessarily mean you have been conditioned.

144
00:14:53,686 --> 00:14:55,946
Maybe you just like my videos.

145
00:14:56,426 --> 00:15:05,099
A sure telltale sign that you have not been mentally manipulated would be the urge to click on "subscribe" and on "share" as well, as those buttons were not part of the experiment.

146
00:15:06,581 --> 00:15:09,331
Please don't think I'm trying to manipulate you.

147
00:15:09,657 --> 00:15:11,217
That's just not my style.

148
00:15:12,137 --> 00:15:14,529
This was the ninth episode of Earthlings 101.

149
00:15:15,056 --> 00:15:24,176
Next time we'll talk about language: How it works, how it developed, and why the causes of its apparent decline - are actually the very forces that build it.

150
00:15:24,796 --> 00:15:28,538
Thanks for watching, and as always: Don't forget to be alien!

